= Solution
{parent=i}

For $x,y\in B$, write $L_x(y)=xy$ and $R_y(x)=xy$. Separate <continuity> and <linearity> make both maps <bounded linear operators>. For each fixed $y$,
$$
\sup_{\|x\|\leq1}\|L_x y\|=\sup_{\|x\|\leq1}\|R_yx\|\leq\|R_y\|<\infty.
$$
The <uniform boundedness principle> therefore gives $C<\infty$ such that $\|L_x\|\leq C$ whenever $\|x\|\leq1$. Scaling yields
$$
\|xy\|\leq C\|x\|\|y\|.
$$
For completeness, the relevant <uniform boundedness principle> follows from the <Baire category theorem> as follows. For a pointwise bounded family $\mathcal T$ of <bounded linear operators> on a <Banach space>, the <closed sets> $E_m=\{y:\sup_{T\in\mathcal T}\|Ty\|\leq m\}$ cover the space. One $E_m$ contains an <open ball> $B(y_0,r)$. Subtracting the bounds for $y_0$ and $y_0+h$, with $\|h\|<r$, gives $\sup_T\|Th\|\leq2m$. Applying this to $h=(r/2)u$, $\|u\|\leq1$, bounds every <operator norm> by $4m/r$.

Define the new <norm> by
$$
\boxed{\|x\|_* =\|L_x\|.}
$$
It is a <norm> because $L_xe=x$, so $L_x=0$ implies $x=0$. The <triangle inequality> and scalar homogeneity follow from those of the <operator norm>. Moreover,
$$
\frac{\|x\|}{\|e\|}\leq\|L_x\|\leq C\|x\|,
$$
so it is equivalent to the original <norm> and remains complete. By <associativity>, $L_{xy}=L_xL_y$, and the <submultiplicativity> of the <operator norm> proves
$$
\boxed{\|xy\|_*\leq\|x\|_*\|y\|_*.}
$$
This is <renorming a separately continuous Banach algebra>. It even gives $\|e\|_*=1$. If the algebra is the zero space, the conclusion is immediate separately.

= ii
{parent=3}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

A complex <Banach algebra> is an associative complex algebra with a complete <norm> satisfying <submultiplicativity>, $\|ab\|\leq\|a\|\|b\|$. A <field> has a nonzero identity $e$, and every nonzero element is invertible. We derive the <Gelfand-Mazur theorem> from these facts.

If $\|z\|<1$, the series $e+z+z^2+\cdots$ converges in the complete algebra. Multiplying its partial sums by $e-z$ and passing to the limit proves the <Neumann series> inverse. In particular, for any $x\in B$ and $|\lambda|>\|x\|$,
$$
R(\lambda)=(\lambda e-x)^{-1}
=\frac{e}{\lambda}+\sum_{n=1}^{\infty}\frac{x^n}{\lambda^{n+1}}.
$$
This <resolvent of an element> tends to zero in <norm> at infinity; the estimate follows from the geometric bound on $\|x^n\|$.

We next prove that the <spectrum of an element> cannot be empty. Suppose every $\lambda e-x$ were invertible. At each $\lambda_0$, write
$$
\lambda e-x=(\lambda_0e-x)\big(e+(\lambda-\lambda_0)R(\lambda_0)\big).
$$
The <Neumann series> on a small disk about $\lambda_0$ proves that $R$ is analytic there. Consequently, for each <continuous linear functional> $\ell:B\to\mathbb C$, the function $\lambda\mapsto\ell(R(\lambda))$ is entire. It is bounded outside a large disk by the estimate above and bounded on that disk by <continuity>. The <Liouville theorem> makes it constant; its zero limit at infinity makes the constant zero.

Continuous complex <linear functionals> separate points of $B$. Here is the needed consequence of the <Hahn-Banach theorem>: for $z\ne0$, define $g(tz)=t\|z\|$ on the real span of $z$ and extend it as a bounded real <linear functional> of <norm> one. Then $\ell(w)=g(w)-ig(iw)$ is complex linear and continuous, and $\operatorname{Re}\ell(z)=\|z\|\ne0$. The real extension theorem is proved explicitly in Question 4(i) below. Thus $\ell(R(\lambda))=0$ for every $\ell$ forces $R(\lambda)=0$, contradicting $(\lambda e-x)R(\lambda)=e\ne0$. This proves the <nonemptiness of the Banach-algebra spectrum>.

Choose $\lambda\in\sigma_B(x)$. Since $B$ is a <field>, the noninvertible element $x-\lambda e$ must be zero. Hence every $x\in B$ is a scalar multiple of $e$. The map
$$
\boxed{\Phi:\mathbb C\longrightarrow B,\qquad\Phi(\lambda)=\lambda e}
$$
is therefore a bijective unital complex-algebra homomorphism. It and its inverse are bounded, since $\|\lambda e\|=|\lambda|\|e\|$. Thus it is an isomorphism of <Banach algebras>. If the usual normalization $\|e\|=1$ is included in the definition, it is an isometry; without that normalization, it is still the required continuous algebra isomorphism.

= iii
{parent=3}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iii}

**The first algebra exists.** Let $A=L^1(0,1)$ with truncated <convolution>
$$
(f*g)(t)=\int_0^t f(s)g(t-s)\,ds.
$$
The <Tonelli theorem> gives
$$
\|f*g\|_1\leq\int_{s,u\geq0,\ s+u\leq1}|f(s)||g(u)|\,ds\,du
\leq\|f\|_1\|g\|_1.
$$
<Commutativity> follows by replacing $s$ with $t-s$; <associativity> follows from the <Fubini theorem> applied to the simplex $s,u\geq0$, $s+u\leq t$. Together with completeness of $L^1$, this makes $A$ a commutative <Banach algebra>, not necessarily unital. Adjoin an identity using <unitization>:
$$
B=\mathbb C\oplus A,\quad
(\alpha,f)(\beta,g)=(\alpha\beta,\alpha g+\beta f+f*g),\quad
\|(\alpha,f)\|=|\alpha|+\|f\|_1.
$$
This <norm> is complete and submultiplicative by the preceding bound, and $e=(1,0)$ is the identity. This is the <Volterra convolution algebra on a finite interval> with an identity adjoined.

Take $x=(0,\mathbf1)$, where $\mathbf1(t)=1$. Direct induction under <convolution> gives
$$
x^n=(0,t^{n-1}/(n-1)!),\qquad
\|x^n\|=\frac1{n!}>0.
$$
Thus no power vanishes. For every $\lambda\ne0$ the series
$$
\frac e\lambda+\sum_{n=1}^{\infty}\frac{x^n}{\lambda^{n+1}}
$$
converges absolutely, since its <norms> have factorial denominators. Multiplication by $\lambda e-x$ telescopes to $e$, so it is an inverse. On the other hand $x$ cannot be invertible: its scalar coordinate is zero, and scalar coordinates multiply, so $xy$ can never equal $e$. Hence
$$
\boxed{\sigma_B(x)=\{0\},\qquad \rho(x)=0,\qquad x^n\ne0\text{ for every }n\geq1.}
$$
This explicitly exhibits a nonnilpotent <quasinilpotent element> without assuming a spectral-radius formula.

**The second algebra does not exist.** Suppose it did, and choose a nonscalar $y$. Nonemptiness of its <spectrum of an element>, together with $\rho(y)=0$, gives $\sigma_B(y)=\{0\}$. But $y+e$ is still nonscalar: if $y+e=\alpha e$, then $y=(\alpha-1)e$. By <translation of the spectrum by a scalar>,
$$
\sigma_B(y+e)=1+\sigma_B(y)=\{1\},\qquad\rho(y+e)=1.
$$
This contradicts the required zero <spectral radius> for every nonscalar element. The contradiction already uses the spectral condition alone; the nonvanishing-power condition cannot remedy it.

